const takeaway = `
<p>The copy is not wrong, it is just answering a different question. It builds the
answer in a second array and pastes it over the original at the end, which means
<code>nums</code> holds garbage until the very last pass and the <code>O(n)</code> of
extra memory is exactly the thing the problem asked you not to spend.</p>

<p>The two-pointer version turns on one idea: <code>slow</code> is not really an index
into the input, it is a <strong>boundary</strong>. Everything to its left is already
correct and will never be touched again; everything between <code>slow</code> and
<code>fast</code> is zeroes waiting to be swapped away; everything past
<code>fast</code> has not been looked at. Each non-zero value <code>fast</code> finds
gets swapped down to the boundary and the boundary moves up by one, which keeps that
sentence true after every iteration. When <code>fast</code> runs off the end the
sentence still holds, and a true sentence about the whole array is the answer.</p>

<p>Naming the invariant is what makes this family of problems readable rather than a
pile of index arithmetic. The same boundary appears in
<strong>Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array</strong>, where the prefix left of
<code>slow</code> is the deduplicated answer, and in <strong>Sort Colors</strong>, where
two boundaries fence off the 0s at the front and the 2s at the back. Once you can state
what is true to the left of the pointer, the loop body usually writes itself.</p>

<p>On the follow-up: this version writes only when it finds a non-zero value, so an
array of mostly zeroes costs very few swaps, and an array of no zeroes costs
<code>n</code> swaps of a cell with itself. If those self-swaps bother you, write
<code>nums[slow] = nums[fast]</code> and zero out the tail afterwards — same order,
fewer writes when the array is dense, at the cost of a second loop.</p>`;
